\begin_layout Subsection
Why are experts explainable? Human vs.
 Machine
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The human brain learning architecture has been perfected in many ways through
 an evolutionary process lasting millions of years, which allows learning
 a wide range of skills in a very diverse learning process.
 while being severely limited in time and exploration compared to machines.
 Image recognition, for example, is perfected in early childhood to such
 an extent that it hardly needs any explanation.
 Humans are also as lazy as they can be and, instead of making an effort
 to find new concepts for a problem, they try to solve a problem with what
 they have already learned.
 Language, as well as the ability to speak, is learned as a child and at
 the same time describes everything that a person can understand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
The limits of my language mean the limits of my world.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
(Ludwig Wittgenstein, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Die Grenzen meiner Sprache bedeuten die Grenzen meiner Welt.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wittgenstein1953philosophical"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interpersonal communication and the ability to implement successful models
 in all people (such as the Pythagorean theorem) also avoids over- and underfitt
ing as much as possible.
 As a communication protocol, a language works because the experiences between
 people are similar enough to be learned and comparable.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Let's consider, for example, the game Puzzle as an NP-hard problem, where
 a whole picture has to be assembled from a pile of pieces.
 Most people know how to recognize colors, that you first have to turn puzzle
 pieces to the colored side and that you should use the picture on the box
 as a reference point.
 A popular tip for beginners is therefore that you should start with the
 edge of the puzzle.
 This is helpful, because the learning process in humans usually consists
 of the composition of many comprehensibly strung together steps.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

